---

<BaseLayout
  title="Modal · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Native HTML modal using dialog and command/commandfor, styled with Bootstrap 5.3.8."
>
  <h1>Modal</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Use Bootstrap’s modal markup on a native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>. Open
    and close with <code>command</code> / <code>commandfor</code> — no Modal
    plugin.
  </p>

  <DocsMeta id="modal" at="start" />

  <h2 class="h4">How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>command="show-modal"</code> and <code>commandfor="id"</code> replace
      <code>data-bs-toggle="modal"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>command="close"</code> replaces <code>data-bs-dismiss="modal"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The browser draws the backdrop as <code>::backdrop</code>. Fade and slide
      use CSS transitions and <code>@starting-style</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Default live dialogs use <code>closedby=&quot;any&quot;</code> so
      backdrop click and Escape request close.
      <code>closedby=&quot;none&quot;</code> replaces a static backdrop.
    </li>
    <li>
      Native events on the dialog are <code>close</code> and
      <code>cancel</code> instead of <code>*.bs.modal</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code> inside the dialog
      closes it on submit. Each submit button’s
      <code>value</code> becomes <code>dialog.returnValue</code> — a
      confirm or prompt without script. Read that property on the
      <code>close</code> event if you need it in the page.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Example
    title="Modal components"
    lead="A static example of the modal header, body, and footer. This preview is not a dialog — use the live demo to open one."
    bg="body-tertiary"
    code={componentsCode}
  />

  <Example title="Live demo" code={liveDemoCode} />

  <Example
    title="Static backdrop"
    lead="closedby=&quot;none&quot; prevents closing on backdrop click and Escape. Dismiss with the close control instead."
    code={staticBackdropCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Scrolling long content"
    lead="When modals become too long for the viewport, they scroll independent of the page itself."
    code={scrollingCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Scrollable modal body"
    lead="Add .modal-dialog-scrollable to keep the header and footer visible while the body scrolls."
    code={scrollableCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Vertically centered"
    lead="Add .modal-dialog-centered to center the modal vertically."
    code={centeredCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Using the grid"
    lead="Utilize the Bootstrap grid system within a modal by nesting .container-fluid within the .modal-body."
    code={gridCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Toggle between modals"
    lead="Open another dialog with commandfor."
    code={toggleCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      Bootstrap’s Modal plugin shows only one modal at a time and hides the
      current one when another opens. Native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>
      elements stack instead — there is no way to auto-hide the first
      dialog without JavaScript.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Optional sizes"
    lead="Modals have three optional sizes via modifier classes on .modal-dialog. These sizes kick in at certain breakpoints."
    code={sizesCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Fullscreen modal"
    lead="Cover the viewport with modifier classes on .modal-dialog."
    code={fullscreenCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Confirm with method=dialog"
    lead="The form wraps the modal shell. Cancel, Delete, and the close control are submit buttons. No command=close on those controls."
    code={dialogFormCode}
  />

  <h3>returnValue</h3>
  <p>
    Open the dialog, pick a button, then read
    <code>dialog.returnValue</code> on <code>close</code>. This output
    is a docs demo — the markup above needs no script.
  </p>
  <figure class="example-figure mb-5">
    <div class="example-preview">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="btn btn-primary"
        command="show-modal"
        commandfor="dialogReturnModal"
      >
        Launch confirm
      </button>
      <p class="small text-body-secondary mt-3 mb-0">
        Last returnValue:
        <output id="dialog-return-out">—</output>
      </p>
      <dialog
        class="modal"
        closedby="any"
        id="dialogReturnModal"
        aria-labelledby="dialogReturnModalLabel"
      >
        <form method="dialog">
          <div class="modal-dialog">
            <div class="modal-content">
              <div class="modal-header">
                <h1 class="modal-title fs-5" id="dialogReturnModalLabel">
                  Delete file?
                </h1>
                <button
                  type="submit"
                  class="btn-close"
                  value="cancel"
                  aria-label="Close"
                ></button>
              </div>
              <div class="modal-body">
                Choose Delete or Cancel. Backdrop or Escape leaves
                returnValue empty.
              </div>
              <div class="modal-footer">
                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary" value="cancel">
                  Cancel
                </button>
                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-danger" value="delete">
                  Delete
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </form>
      </dialog>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <script is:inline>
    const dialog = document.getElementById("dialogReturnModal");
    const out = document.getElementById("dialog-return-out");
    if (dialog && out) {
      dialog.addEventListener("close", () => {
        out.textContent = dialog.returnValue === "" ? "(empty)" : dialog.returnValue;
      });
    }
  </script>

  <DocsMeta id="modal" at="end" />
</BaseLayout>
